# quick pathophysiology (cover): ADN level (Ignatavicius 11e terms), plain words, no chart numbers, nothing that gives away a later stop
PATHO_TITLE = 'QUICK PATHOPHYSIOLOGY: TYPE 1 DIABETES AND DIABETIC KETOACIDOSIS (DKA)'
PATHO = [('The pancreas and insulin', 'Beta cells in the pancreas make **insulin**. Insulin lets glucose into the cells for fuel, stops the liver from making more glucose, stops fat from breaking down into ketones, and moves potassium into the cells.'),
         ('Type 1 diabetes', 'The immune system destroys the beta cells, so the body makes little or no insulin. **Too little insulin:** high glucose, thirst (polydipsia), urinating (polyuria), hunger (polyphagia) with weight loss, tiredness, blurred vision. **Too much insulin** (a dose bigger than the need): low glucose, with shakiness, sweating or confusion.'),
         ('What tips her into DKA', 'With no insulin, glucose piles up and spills into the urine, pulling water and electrolytes with it (**osmotic diuresis**: dehydration). The body burns fat and makes **ketones**, which are acids (**metabolic acidosis**). Vomiting makes both worse.'),
         ('Main dangers', 'Shock from dehydration; dysrhythmias from **potassium shifts** (acid and no insulin push potassium out of the cells, so the blood level can look high while the body total is low); low glucose and low potassium once treatment starts; brain swelling (rare, mostly in young people).'),
         ('Treatment logic', '**Fluids first** to restore circulation (the corrected sodium then guides which saline). **IV regular insulin** at a fixed rate, started only once the potassium is known, to stop ketone production. **Potassium** replaced as insulin drives it into the cells (a peripheral IV takes no more than 10 mEq an hour). **Dextrose is added** when glucose falls, so the insulin keeps running until the ketones clear. Bicarbonate only for a very low pH.'),
         ('Nursing focus', 'Heart rhythm and vital signs, the depth of breathing, glucose every hour, potassium, anion gap and ketones, mental status, intake and output, IV sites and fall safety. Then teach the survival skills a person with new diabetes needs to go home safely.')]

# concept map (page 2): what students fill at 0700
MAP = [('INSULIN (missing)', 'Lets glucose into cells; stops the liver making glucose; stops fat breaking down into ketones.'),
       ('GLUCOSE IN THE URINE (osmotic diuresis)', 'Kidneys normally keep all glucose. Above about 180 it spills into the urine and pulls water and salts with it.'),
       ('KETONES (acid)', 'Normally few. Without insulin, fat is burned and ketoacids build up (metabolic acidosis). The lungs blow off CO2 to compensate; acetone smells fruity.'),
       ('POTASSIUM (shifts)', 'Mostly inside the cells. Acid and no insulin push it out into the blood; urine loses it; insulin moves it back in.')]
MAP_CHAIN = ('No insulin -> high glucose -> osmotic diuresis -> dehydration.   No insulin -> fat burned -> ketones -> metabolic acidosis -> Kussmaul breathing.   '
             'Acid + no insulin -> potassium out of the cells (insulin pushes it back in).')

# Quick Check helper: step = clinical judgment step; kind mc (4 options) / sata (select all) / fill; ok = correct letters (key only)
def Q(step, q, kind, opts, ok, ans, why, wrong, topic):
    return dict(step=step, q=q, kind=kind, opts=opts, ok=ok, ans=ans, why=why, wrong=wrong, topic=topic)

